  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nSurface high pressure will linger over the Quad State today into\nTuesday, then build to the east, allowing south winds to return\nto the region Wednesday. Temperatures will start out a few\ndegrees below normal today and then trend upward through\nWednesday and Thursday, when highs in the upper 80s to around \n90 degrees are expected. The NBM has a 30-50% chance of \ntemperatures reaching at least 90 on Wednesday, and a 30-90% \nchance on Thursday. The best chances will generally be in the \nsouth and east each day.\n\nGuidance continues to struggle with the strength of mixing,\ntherefore temperatures may outperform the NBM numbers today and\nTuesday. Expect relative humidity to drop into the 20-30% range\nover much of the region both days. Thankfully, winds will \nremain light, so fire danger should remain in check.\n\nA storm system moving through Ontario on Thursday will push a\ncold front through the Quad State Thursday night into early \nFriday. The NBM has a 40-60% chance of showers near the front, \nas it passes through the region, but instability will be very \nlimited, so no thunder or heavy rainfall is expected. \n\nThe forecast for the weekend and beyond is quite murky, and \nappears to be in flux at this time. The 00Z EC and GFS have a\nsimilar scenario that goes like this. The southern stream \nsystem now shears out over the southern Plains Friday into \nSaturday. Some of its energy will stream over the Quad State \nFriday night and Saturday producing widespread showers. Then \na northern stream system will descend on the Great Lakes and \npush another cold front through the area, bringing an end to \nthe rain Saturday evening. \n\nAs always the NBM lags the trend, and that may not be a bad \nthing in this situation, with influences from multiple streams \naloft. This NBM-based forecast still has PoPs beginning \nThursday and continuing every period through next Monday. \n\nTemperatures will drop back below normal behind Thursday night's\ncold front and will then trend even cooler Sunday and Monday.",
